-- js-kinds.rofl — a DELIBERATELY TINY sample, enough to test the mechanism and
-- not one node more. The real vocabulary is babel's 252 concrete types; this is
-- eight of them plus five Python counterparts, because the question here is
-- whether ADDING A LAYER IS ONE FACT, not what any cell contains.
edb(node_kind).
edb(layer).
edb(handled).
edb(ignored).

node_kind(js, call_expression).
node_kind(js, if_statement).
node_kind(js, assignment_expression).
node_kind(js, function_declaration).
node_kind(js, import_declaration).
node_kind(js, ts_string_keyword).
node_kind(js, yield_expression).
node_kind(js, import_expression).
node_kind(js, variable_declarator).

node_kind(py, call).
node_kind(py, if_stmt).
node_kind(py, assign).
node_kind(py, function_def).
node_kind(py, import_from).

-- TWO layers to begin with. The third is added later, as ONE FACT, and nothing
-- else may change: that is the whole experiment.
layer(dataflow).
layer(callgraph).

-- what is modelled so far, per (language, kind, layer)
handled(js, call_expression,       dataflow,  r_call_args_flow).
handled(js, call_expression,       callgraph, r_call_edge).
handled(js, assignment_expression, dataflow,  r_assign_flow).
handled(js, function_declaration,  callgraph, r_callee_target).
handled(py, call,                  dataflow,  r_call_args_flow).
handled(py, call,                  callgraph, r_call_edge).
handled(py, assign,                dataflow,  r_assign_flow).
handled(py, function_def,          callgraph, r_callee_target).

-- and what is deliberately out, WITH A REASON. Silence is not an option.
ignored(js, ts_string_keyword, dataflow,  a_type_only_no_runtime_value).
ignored(js, ts_string_keyword, callgraph, a_type_only_no_runtime_value).
ignored(js, if_statement,      dataflow,  a_control_not_data).
ignored(py, if_stmt,           dataflow,  a_control_not_data).

-- модель, дописанная тиком 3
handled(js, variable_declarator, dataflow, r_declarator_init_flow).
ignored(js, if_statement, callgraph, a_no_call_edge_from_a_conditional).

-- The ONE cell actually run against an oracle, tick 3: scanner -> rule ->
-- execution with a tagged value, four fixtures, 3 agree and 1 predicted miss.
-- Recorded as EVIDENCE rather than as a claim, which is the difference the
-- tautology above was hiding.
checked(js, variable_declarator, dataflow, r_declarator_init_flow, o_run_with_tag, 4).


-- TYPED REASONS for cells that are not modelled. Every other not_modelled cell
-- takes the default `not_yet` from the rule, so this file records only the
-- ones where the default would be a LIE — where the unknown belongs to the
-- subject rather than to us.
--
-- `import(specifier)` with a computed specifier: which module is loaded, and
-- therefore what the promise resolves to and which function a later call
-- reaches, is not determined until the program runs. No amount of rule-writing
-- here removes it; only an assumption about resolution would, and an
-- assumption is a different claim.
unknown_because(js, import_expression, dataflow,  runtime_dependent).
unknown_because(js, import_expression, callgraph, runtime_dependent).

-- DECLARED GAP, so it is not mistaken for a complete exercise of the taxonomy:
-- `budget_exhausted` and `out_of_scope` have no honest row in this file yet —
-- no evaluation here has hit a bound, and everything deliberately outside the
-- model is already a waiver with a reason (`ignored`). Both atoms are
-- exercised in test/js-model.test.ts instead. Writing a false row to make the
-- census look full is the failure this whole file exists to prevent.

-- ===========================================================================
-- THE THIRD AXIS IS NOT IN THIS FILE, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE.
--
-- `axis(shape)`, `axis_applies/2` and `shape_of/3` live in facts/js-shapes.rofl.
-- They could be written here — the rules that read them are in
-- rules/js-model.rofl, loaded by everything — and putting them here would be
-- wrong for a measured reason rather than a tidiness one.
--
-- This file is the MECHANISM fixture: 14 kinds, 2 layers, 28 cells, pinned
-- number for number in test/js-model.test.ts because the question it answers
-- is "does adding a layer take one fact", not "what does any cell contain".
-- Not one of its kinds has a shape. Declaring `axis_applies(shape, callgraph)`
-- here would open 28 shaped-but-unsplit cells over a fixture with no shapes in
-- it, which is an axis applied to a layer it cannot say anything about — the
-- exact defect `unearned_axis[audit]` was written to report, planted in the
-- fixture that is supposed to be clean.
--
-- The shape axis therefore loads on top of this file together with
-- facts/js-callgraph.rofl, which is where the kinds it splits are declared.
-- Two worlds, and test/js-model.test.ts builds both.

-- ===========================================================================
-- THE FRAME, decided 2026-09-06 — queue item w_vocabulary_frame.
--
-- `vocabulary_gap[audit]` stood at SEVENTEEN on an honest checkout: kinds the
-- scanner emits that `node_kind` does not declare. Measured, they are not one
-- list but THREE, and calling them one is what made the item look like a chore
-- rather than the decision its own note said it was.

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- NOT CONSTRUCTS. Two are the scanner's own roots and one is trivia. No layer
-- can have an opinion about any of them; declaring them would produce twelve
-- waivers that describe the SCANNER rather than the language, and a waiver that
-- describes the instrument is noise in a matrix that exists to describe JS.
not_a_construct(file).
not_a_construct(program).
not_a_construct(comment_line).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- FOUR REAL CONSTRUCTS, declared and answered. `binary_expression` was already
-- LOAD-BEARING before it was declared — facts/js-env.rofl carries
-- `kind_baseline(js, binary_expression)` and `attr_needs(js, binary_expression,
-- operator, "**", exponentiation)`, so the era layer has been reasoning about a
-- kind the vocabulary did not contain. That is the sharpest instance of what
-- this audit exists to catch.
-- ...and the four `node_kind` rows are NOT here either, for the second half of
-- the same reason. This file is the minimal world test/js-model.test.ts uses to
-- test the MATRIX MACHINERY — boot plus these facts and nothing else — so a
-- kind declared here with its verdicts elsewhere adds cells that world can never
-- answer: `not_modelled` went 14 -> 22 and every pin in that file moved. The
-- convention in force is `a file that makes a claim declares the kind`, and this
-- file makes no claim about these four. See the four layer packs.

-- THE SIXTEEN VERDICTS ARE NOT HERE. `orphan_claim[audit]` reported eight of
-- them within one run: a claim about a cell that does not exist, because a
-- world that declares `layer(callgraph)` and not `layer(modules)` has no cell
-- for a modules verdict — and this file is loaded in every world. A verdict
-- belongs in the pack that DECLARES ITS LAYER, which is the sentence
-- w_vocabulary_home is about and the reason the kind rows get copied.
-- See facts/js-callgraph.rofl, js-dataflow.rofl, js-modules.rofl and
-- js-controlflow.rofl for the four rows each of these kinds carries.

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- THE TEN TYPE NODES ARE DECLARED, 2026-09-07, and the deferral that stood here
-- was wrong about which question they belong to.
--
-- WHAT IT SAID: "Declaring them costs FORTY cells that no rule can close,
-- because what a type MEANS in this model is w_type_surface's question and that
-- item is blocked behind the API surface."
--
-- WHAT A TYPE NODE MEANS AT THESE FOUR LAYERS IS NOT THAT QUESTION AT ALL. A
-- `tstype_annotation` carries no runtime value, is never a callee, transfers no
-- control, and is not a module construct — four exact facts about the LANGUAGE,
-- the same class as `binary_expression`'s four, and the reason atom
-- `a_type_only_no_runtime_value` had already been invented for exactly them.
-- The deferral confused A NODE IN TYPE POSITION with THE TYPE OF A VALUE. They
-- share a word and nothing else: `xs.at(-1)` needs to know that `xs` is an
-- Array, and no `tstype_annotation` anywhere says so.
--
-- SEVENTH DESIGN NOTE IN THIS LOOP REFUTED BY A PROBE, and the first one where
-- what was refuted was my own pricing of a decision rather than a claim about
-- the store: forty unanswerable cells were the reason for the deferral, and
-- forty answerable cells is what they were.
--
-- The `node_kind` rows are in the four layer packs, with their verdicts, for
-- the same reason the frame's four are: a file that makes a claim declares the
-- kind, and a verdict belongs in the pack that declares its layer.

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- DECLARED AND NEVER PRODUCED, WITH THE REASON WRITTEN DOWN.
--
-- `kind_unexercised[audit]` demands a row here for a kind this vocabulary
-- declares and this corpus does not contain. It is NOT an error — a vocabulary
-- may be wider than any one corpus, exactly as `feature_unexercised` may be —
-- but the reason has to be sayable, and writing it is what found the first
-- entry below.
kind_absent_ok(ts_string_keyword, a_synthetic_fixture_the_scanner_cannot_emit).
-- ...and the other SEVEN rows are not here, for the reason `orphan_claim` gives
-- about a verdict and `kind_absent_undeclared[audit]` gave about these within
-- one run: a row belongs in the pack that DECLARES its kind, or a world loading
-- one pack and not the other carries an excuse for something it has never heard
-- of. See the four layer packs.
--
-- THE FIRST ROW IS THE ONE THAT PAID FOR THE AUDIT. `ts_string_keyword` is not
-- a kind: the scanner snake-cases babel's `TSStringKeyword` to
-- `tsstring_keyword`, with no underscore after `ts`, so nothing will ever
-- produce this spelling. It has carried four verdicts across four packs since
-- the vocabulary was written, all of them about a kind that does not exist,
-- while the REAL spelling sat in the deferral list — so a reader asking whether
-- TypeScript's string keyword was handled found a row saying yes. It stays,
-- because two mutants in test/js-model.test.ts use it as a kind with a verdict
-- and it is honestly declared now; what changed is that its status is a ROW
-- instead of a comment in a different file. facts/js-env.rofl had already
-- noticed and routed around it, gating both spellings with a note calling this
-- one "a SYNTHETIC fixture with no corpus behind it" — a known oddity nobody
-- had anywhere to record.
